WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:02.680
Let's go instead to Tyler from St. Louis.

00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:04.900
Tyler, you're on the air.

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:10.650
Hello, David, um, so.

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:14.310
I don't know exactly the subject of everything,
but.

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:20.869
I was wondering, is there anything to the
arguments that universal health care would

00:00:20.869 --> 00:00:24.359
lead to lower quality of health care?

00:00:24.359 --> 00:00:31.710
Well, that's what I tend to hear, but I never
complain, never explain causal relationships.

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:35.410
They're the best the best of the best stuff
I've read about.

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:44.460
This is from the late Ouvea Rhinehart, who
wrote that basically imagine a triangle.

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:48.929
You're familiar with a triangle, I'm sure,
and you know that it has three corners.

00:00:48.929 --> 00:00:50.059
Does that make sense?

00:00:50.059 --> 00:00:51.440
Mm hmm.

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:59.269
The three corners of health care are cost,
quality and access, and that no matter what

00:00:59.269 --> 00:01:05.580
system you support, you are basically picking
a midpoint between those three.

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:12.630
So, for example, if you want to make it really
cheap and give it to everybody, quality is

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:14.140
going to go down now.

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:18.341
It could still be better quality than we have
and we might still be spending more, you know,

00:01:18.341 --> 00:01:19.390
like we spend a lot.

00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:22.380
So this is just theoretical on building the
theoretical framework.

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:25.890
So if you want to give it to everybody and
make it cheap, there's going to be some sacrifice

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:28.680
in terms of quality, all else being equal.

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:35.619
If you want to make it cheap and really good,
then to some degree you have to restrict access,

00:01:35.619 --> 00:01:36.619
you understand.

00:01:36.619 --> 00:01:38.250
So everything is kind of a balancing act.

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:45.680
And so when we talk about universal health
care, if you want it to be universal and you

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:49.700
want it to be really good, it's just going
to be more expensive.

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:53.020
Now, a lot of people will hear me say this
and they'll say, David, that's not true.

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:57.259
Look at how much we spend on medical billing
and there's profit motive.

00:01:57.259 --> 00:01:59.229
And then there's all these levels of bureaucracy.

00:01:59.229 --> 00:02:03.189
And then because some people don't have health
insurance, hospitals raise prices and make

00:02:03.189 --> 00:02:04.530
them artificially high.

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:05.890
That's all completely true.

00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:08.119
That's absolutely and completely true.

00:02:08.119 --> 00:02:13.610
I'm just saying, all else being equal, if
you want to make it high quality and available

00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:15.570
to everybody, it's going to cost some money.

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:18.380
And it may be it may be a reasonable amount
of money.

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:25.010
For example, I would be OK with it being expensive
if we've actually eliminated all of the waste

00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:27.250
and the bureaucracy and all of these things.

00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:30.220
It's just as a decision as a society that
we have to make.

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:33.190
And so that's the sort of triangle you have
to think of.

00:02:33.190 --> 00:02:38.000
It's all just picking a point between those
three corners and saying, here is where we

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:39.470
want to be.

00:02:39.470 --> 00:02:42.800
OK, thank you.

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:47.520
And by the way, if you ever end up in St.
Louis, make sure to try St. Louis style pizza.

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:48.890
Is that a thing or is that a joke?

00:02:48.890 --> 00:02:49.960
Well, it's the thing.

00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:51.170
It's really good.

00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:53.750
What it what what are the characteristics?

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:58.450
So it has its own we have our own cheese called
Proval, OK?

00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:05.850
And it's like it's like a mix of provolone
and Swiss and American cheese.

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:07.230
People say it's an acquired taste, but I don't
know.

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:08.650
I've always liked it.

00:03:08.650 --> 00:03:10.700
Well, I'm up for trying it.

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:11.700
Yeah.

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:12.700
And then it's cut.

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:14.390
It's cut into squares.

00:03:14.390 --> 00:03:15.570
And what about that?

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:19.900
And it's got a very it's got a very thin,
like cracker like crust.

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:20.900
OK.

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:21.900
All right.

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:24.500
Well, the next time I'm in St. Louis, I will
check that out.

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:25.850
All right, see you.

00:03:25.850 --> 00:03:41.020
All right, my friend, thank you 

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:50.600
for the call, appreciate it.

00:03:50.600 --> 00:04:18.330
Let's go to I think this is either Alexa or
Alexander or Alexandra in Los Angeles.

00:04:18.330 --> 00:04:47.220
Alex in Los Angeles, you're on the air.

